WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.200
Welcome back to Building
Ticket-to-Machine.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.700
This is Chapter 4: drift to a
typed task, the kit's Build 1.

3
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:10.987
A workflow that posts a finding
is easy to build and hard to see.

4
00:00:10.987 --> 00:00:15.415
You can't run a SailPoint workflow on a
laptop, but you can resolve its request

5
00:00:15.415 --> 00:00:19.701
body against a saved event and see the
exact payload ServiceNow would get.

6
00:00:19.701 --> 00:00:23.200
Doing that found two gaps,
and this chapter fixes both.

7
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.940
Then two labs, the
dangers and the checklist.

8
00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:31.615
Why bother?

9
00:00:31.615 --> 00:00:36.528
Because the body of a SailPoint workflow
is full of keys that SailPoint resolves

10
00:00:36.528 --> 00:00:39.314
only at run time, so until
now the only way to see

11
00:00:39.314 --> 00:00:42.540
the actual payload was to
run the workflow in a tenant.

12
00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.953
And what you can't see, you can't test.

13
00:00:44.953 --> 00:00:49.684
If the workflow drops part of an event,
that part of the change is never reverted,

14
00:00:49.684 --> 00:00:52.814
and the re-read later finds
nothing to disagree with.

15
00:00:52.814 --> 00:00:55.604
So this chapter resolves the body offline,

16
00:00:55.604 --> 00:01:01.470
derives the intended state the way ServiceNow
will, and lists anything left behind.

17
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.208
Here's what Build 1 already has.

18
00:01:07.208 --> 00:01:10.033
Three SailPoint workflows,
one per trigger.

19
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:13.707
A native change to an account
raises an unauthorized write.

20
00:01:13.707 --> 00:01:17.544
A change to an identity's
attributes raises attribute drift.

21
00:01:17.544 --> 00:01:21.301
And a completed provisioning
action raises a closure signal,

22
00:01:21.301 --> 00:01:23.657
which never closes anything by itself.

23
00:01:23.657 --> 00:01:27.909
Each workflow has one HTTP request
step and is created disabled,

24
00:01:27.909 --> 00:01:30.796
because SailPoint won't
create one enabled.

25
00:01:30.796 --> 00:01:35.883
On the ServiceNow side, the scripted REST
resource derives the intended state:

26
00:01:35.883 --> 00:01:39.840
a native change is reverted,
an attribute change is kept.

27
00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:45.578
The new tool does five things.

28
00:01:45.578 --> 00:01:47.940
It picks the workflow
for the trigger you name.

29
00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.930
It resolves the body against a saved
event, but only simple paths; anything

30
00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:58.165
fancier is refused, because it isn't a
JSON path engine and won't pretend to be.

31
00:01:58.165 --> 00:02:01.853
It derives the intended state
the same way ServiceNow will.

32
00:02:01.853 --> 00:02:04.802
It checks the result against
Chapter 1's contract.

33
00:02:04.802 --> 00:02:09.265
And it lists every top-level field of
the event that the body never reads.

34
00:02:09.265 --> 00:02:14.460
Three fictional events, shaped like
SailPoint's published examples, feed it.

35
00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:19.335
The first fix.

36
00:02:19.335 --> 00:02:24.748
SailPoint's native change event has three
kinds of change: single-value attributes,

37
00:02:24.748 --> 00:02:29.847
entitlements, and multivalued attributes,
each with values added and removed.

38
00:02:29.847 --> 00:02:32.198
The workflow sent only the first two.

39
00:02:32.198 --> 00:02:36.057
So a native change that touched
only a multivalued attribute,

40
00:02:36.057 --> 00:02:40.371
a proxy address added out of band,
say, produced an intended state with

41
00:02:40.371 --> 00:02:44.609
nothing in it, and a re-read would
have found nothing to disagree with.

42
00:02:44.609 --> 00:02:49.653
Now the workflow sends the multivalued
changes, and the script reverts them:

43
00:02:49.653 --> 00:02:53.280
added values must be absent,
removed ones present.

44
00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.918
The second fix is in
the de-duplication key.

45
00:02:59.918 --> 00:03:04.518
ServiceNow folded findings together
on source, subject and class.

46
00:03:04.518 --> 00:03:07.372
So when two attributes
drifted on one user,

47
00:03:07.372 --> 00:03:10.554
department and manager,
they became one record.

48
00:03:10.554 --> 00:03:13.412
The second finding's
intended state was lost,

49
00:03:13.412 --> 00:03:15.805
and the re-read checked only the first.

50
00:03:15.805 --> 00:03:19.642
Now the key adds the attribute
whenever the payload names one.

51
00:03:19.642 --> 00:03:22.173
The slide shows the key for a department

52
00:03:22.173 --> 00:03:24.856
change on Erin's identity,
before and after.

53
00:03:24.856 --> 00:03:28.700
Each drifting attribute is
now its own piece of work.

54
00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:33.912
Both fixes are pinned.

55
00:03:33.912 --> 00:03:37.332
Seven Python tests run each
workflow against its event,

56
00:03:37.332 --> 00:03:41.049
check that a native change
reverts all three kinds of change,

57
00:03:41.049 --> 00:03:44.915
reproduce the empty intended
state the old workflow produced,

58
00:03:44.915 --> 00:03:49.748
confirm the attribute change keeps the
new value with the attribute in the key,

59
00:03:49.748 --> 00:03:53.837
and cover the closure signal,
refused paths and an unknown trigger.

60
00:03:53.837 --> 00:03:57.192
Two new Node tests run the
ServiceNow script itself,

61
00:03:57.192 --> 00:04:02.630
and the contract test now requires the
script to read the multivalued changes.

62
00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:09.268
The trigger facts, read on November first.

63
00:04:09.268 --> 00:04:11.793
The native change trigger
is fire-and-forget,

64
00:04:11.793 --> 00:04:16.502
and fires only when an aggregation finds
an account changed directly on the source,

65
00:04:16.502 --> 00:04:19.914
with native change detection
on, account updates monitored,

66
00:04:19.914 --> 00:04:21.893
and a monitored attribute changed.

67
00:04:21.893 --> 00:04:25.367
Its trigger ID isn't in
SailPoint's published table.

68
00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:29.014
The attribute-change and
provisioning triggers are listed,

69
00:04:29.014 --> 00:04:31.930
both fire-and-forget, with
the inputs on the slide.

70
00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:37.035
A workflow can't be created enabled, creating
one needs the workflow manage scope,

71
00:04:37.035 --> 00:04:40.280
and the ServiceNow side
needs an endpoint ACL.

72
00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:46.556
Several things are open,
and they matter because

73
00:04:46.556 --> 00:04:48.955
they're exactly what the
offline tool assumes.

74
00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:52.718
The native change trigger's ID
isn't in SailPoint's table.

75
00:04:52.718 --> 00:04:56.186
The HTTP request step's JSON
names aren't published,

76
00:04:56.186 --> 00:05:00.443
and nor is whether it resolves
those run-time keys inside its body.

77
00:05:00.443 --> 00:05:05.017
The shape of each added entitlement
isn't documented below the list level,

78
00:05:05.017 --> 00:05:08.467
and provisioning results
beyond success aren't listed.

79
00:05:08.467 --> 00:05:12.243
Nor is whether the event sits at
the root or under a trigger key.

80
00:05:12.243 --> 00:05:14.880
Lab 2 settles these for your tenant.

81
00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:20.105
What Build 1 proves.

82
00:05:20.105 --> 00:05:24.619
A write made outside the governed process
is detected and raised as an owned,

83
00:05:24.619 --> 00:05:27.701
clocked finding whose
intended state is the revert,

84
00:05:27.701 --> 00:05:31.642
which supports change control and
system monitoring at Moderate.

85
00:05:31.642 --> 00:05:34.954
Each drifting attribute
is its own piece of work.

86
00:05:34.954 --> 00:05:40.918
And a workflow change is tested before it
runs, which is developer testing, SA-11.

87
00:05:40.918 --> 00:05:46.106
Two labels: treating a native change as
an unauthorized write is the program's

88
00:05:46.106 --> 00:05:50.647
inference, and since the workflow
can't know whether a claim is in use,

89
00:05:50.647 --> 00:05:53.100
a person or a rule raises it to P1.

90
00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:57.937
Who does this work?

91
00:05:57.937 --> 00:06:01.160
Identity engineers who build
the SailPoint workflows;

92
00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.924
they read JSON and should run the payload
tool on every change before importing.

93
00:06:05.924 --> 00:06:11.050
ServiceNow developers who own the scripted
REST resource, and deploy both fixes.

94
00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:13.534
And the workflow client from Chapter 3,

95
00:06:13.534 --> 00:06:16.950
with the workflow manage
scope, used once and removed.

96
00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:21.308
One practical point, labeled an
inference: the ServiceNow endpoint has to

97
00:06:21.308 --> 00:06:26.090
be reachable from SailPoint's cloud,
because workflows call out from there.

98
00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:30.652
Lab 1.

99
00:06:30.652 --> 00:06:32.765
First, eleven tests pass.

100
00:06:32.765 --> 00:06:37.153
Then resolve the native-change workflow
against Alice's fictional event.

101
00:06:37.153 --> 00:06:41.139
It's an unauthorized write on
her account, accepted at P2.

102
00:06:41.139 --> 00:06:46.280
The intended state reverts all three
changes: the title goes back to Analyst,

103
00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:50.277
and both the new group and the
new proxy address must be gone.

104
00:06:50.277 --> 00:06:52.795
The only field not sent is the event type,

105
00:06:52.795 --> 00:06:57.065
which is always account-updated for
this trigger, so nothing is lost.

106
00:06:57.065 --> 00:07:00.097
Before the fix, the proxy
address was missing here,

107
00:07:00.097 --> 00:07:03.570
and the multivalued changes
were in that last line.

108
00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:08.808
Next, the mover.

109
00:07:08.808 --> 00:07:12.770
Erin's identity changed
department from HR to Finance.

110
00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:16.476
The finding keeps the new
value as the intended state,

111
00:07:16.476 --> 00:07:20.182
at P3, and the key now ends
in the attribute, department.

112
00:07:20.182 --> 00:07:22.057
Nothing in the event is dropped.

113
00:07:22.057 --> 00:07:25.801
Then the closure signal from a
successful provisioning action:

114
00:07:25.801 --> 00:07:28.132
it's accepted, and four
fields aren't sent.

115
00:07:28.132 --> 00:07:33.695
A signal only moves matching findings to
awaiting verification; it never closes one.

116
00:07:33.695 --> 00:07:38.480
Finally, the two new Node tests of
the ServiceNow script both pass.

117
00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:45.917
Lab 2 needs a SailPoint sandbox
and your developer instance,

118
00:07:45.917 --> 00:07:48.792
never production, and the
authors haven't run it.

119
00:07:48.792 --> 00:07:52.117
Turn on native change
detection on one test source.

120
00:07:52.117 --> 00:07:55.464
Build one HTTP request step
by hand in the editor,

121
00:07:55.464 --> 00:07:58.893
export it, and compare its
names with the kit's file.

122
00:07:58.893 --> 00:08:03.656
Point it at a capture endpoint first,
change a monitored attribute on the source,

123
00:08:03.656 --> 00:08:08.204
and run the saved trigger input through
the tool: the two payloads must match.

124
00:08:08.204 --> 00:08:10.792
Repeat the change and it de-duplicates.

125
00:08:10.792 --> 00:08:12.600
Then put everything back.

126
00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:17.375
The dangers.

127
00:08:17.375 --> 00:08:22.262
An event field the workflow doesn't send
leaves part of a change unreverted,

128
00:08:22.262 --> 00:08:26.125
and the re-read passes anyway;
the tool lists dropped fields.

129
00:08:26.125 --> 00:08:31.375
Two drifts folded into one record lose
an intended state; the key fixes that.

130
00:08:31.375 --> 00:08:34.302
A workflow edited only in the editor stops

131
00:08:34.302 --> 00:08:37.313
matching the repository,
so export and diff.

132
00:08:37.313 --> 00:08:40.100
Credentials in the file
are caught by a test.

133
00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:43.677
A closure signal treated as
closure would skip the re-read,

134
00:08:43.677 --> 00:08:45.912
so it only moves findings to waiting.

135
00:08:45.912 --> 00:08:50.270
And an open endpoint is locked
with authentication and an ACL.

136
00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:55.220
Done looks like this.

137
00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:59.174
Every workflow resolves against a
saved input from your own tenant,

138
00:08:59.174 --> 00:09:01.645
with nothing you meant
to send left behind.

139
00:09:01.645 --> 00:09:05.395
The native-change workflow
sends all three kinds of change.

140
00:09:05.395 --> 00:09:08.007
The deployed script keys on the attribute.

141
00:09:08.007 --> 00:09:11.109
The trigger ID, the step's
attribute names and

142
00:09:11.109 --> 00:09:14.044
the input's root are
confirmed in a sandbox.

143
00:09:14.044 --> 00:09:16.601
Workflows go in disabled, get tested,

144
00:09:16.601 --> 00:09:20.008
then enabled, and the
workflow client is removed.

145
00:09:20.008 --> 00:09:21.421
And the tests pass.

146
00:09:21.421 --> 00:09:26.982
Next, Chapter 5: the re-read rule, and
why a success response isn't closure.

147
00:09:26.982 --> 00:09:28.150
Thanks for watching.
